        <div id="wringers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Spin Mop Wringers Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every spin mop uses the same physics: a round or triangular microfiber head sits in a perforated basket, the basket spins fast, and centrifugal force throws water out through the holes. What differs is how you make it spin. Most buckets now use a foot pedal that you pump with one foot while you hold the handle upright. The original press-down style, used by the Libman Tornado, has you push the handle straight down into the basket to spin it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Foot Pedal Wringers</h3>

          <p>
            Foot pedals are the easier motion for most people. You stand upright, pump a few times and your hands never touch the head. The trade-off is that the pedal and its gear are plastic, and owner reviews of pedal buckets commonly report the pedal or spinner wearing out after heavy use. Push gently and evenly. Stomping the pedal does not wring the head any drier than steady pumps do.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Press-Down Wringers</h3>

          <p>
            Press-down wringers put the effort into your arms and back. Libman's own instructions say to unlock the handle with its release button, then push straight down into the spin chamber; one press leaves the head damp, several leave it drier. Some owners prefer this because there is no pedal to break and no balancing on one foot. Others report that forcing it too hard can strip or snap the spinner, so the same rule applies: firm, not violent.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Dry the Head Gets</h3>

          <p>
            How dry you can get the head matters more than the mechanism. For tile and vinyl, damp is fine. For sealed hardwood and laminate, you want the head barely damp to the touch, which on most spin buckets means eight to ten spins or pumps rather than three. If a mop cannot get there, it is the wrong tool for wood.
          </p>

