        <div id="floors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Mop for Which Floor
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sealed Hardwood</h3>

          <p>
            Sealed hardwood is the floor most often damaged by mopping, and almost always by too much water. Water that sits in the seams between boards can raise the grain, cause cupping and dull the finish. Flooring makers and finish makers consistently advise a damp, not wet, mop and against letting water stand. O-Cedar lists its spin mops as designed for sealed hardwood, but that assumes you wring the head nearly dry and keep moving. Unsealed, waxed or oiled wood should not be wet-mopped at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            For wood, the safest everyday tool is a flat spray mop with a cleaner made for wood finishes. Bona's spray mop puts down a light mist of its water-based hardwood cleaner, and the microfiber pad picks it up before it can pool. Test any cleaner in a closet first, and avoid oil soaps, vinegar mixes and anything that leaves a film, which builds up over time and makes the floor look cloudy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Laminate</h3>

          <p>
            Laminate is even less forgiving than wood at the edges. The core of most laminate is fiberboard, and water that gets into the joints makes it swell, even on planks sold as water resistant. Our explainer on <a href="/is-laminate-flooring-waterproof/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">whether laminate flooring is waterproof</a> covers the difference between the surface and the core. Treat laminate the same way as hardwood: flat mop, light mist, no puddles.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tile, Stone and Vinyl</h3>

          <p>
            Tile, porcelain, sealed stone, vinyl plank and sheet vinyl tolerate far more water, and this is where a spin mop shines. The strand head works into grout lines, and you can run it damper on sticky spots. For natural stone like marble, use a pH-neutral cleaner only, since acidic and alkaline cleaners can etch it. For pet accidents on any hard floor, clean the spot with an enzyme product before mopping; our <a href="/best-enzyme-cleaner/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best enzyme cleaner</a> guide explains why a normal mop pass does not remove the odor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mixed Floors</h3>

          <p>
            If your home is mixed, choose by the floor you mop most. A mostly-tile home with a wood dining room can use a spin mop everywhere if it is wrung hard before the wood. A mostly-wood home with one tiled bathroom should start with a flat mop and handle the bathroom with the same mop and an extra pad.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="head-care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Microfiber Head Care: The Part Everyone Gets Wrong
          </h2>

          <p>
            Microfiber works because its split fibers are fine enough to grab dirt and hold it, rather than pushing it around. That is also why it is easy to ruin. Fabric softener and dryer sheets coat the fibers and fill the gaps, so the head starts gliding over dirt instead of lifting it. Bleach weakens the fibers. O-Cedar's care instructions rule out both for its spin heads, and most microfiber makers say the same.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wash After Every Session</h3>

          <p>
            Wash heads and pads after every full mopping session, not once a week. A damp, dirty head sitting in a closet grows mildew and starts to smell within a day or two, and that smell transfers to the floor next time. Wash warm on a gentle cycle, with a normal dose of detergent, and ideally with other microfiber or on their own, since cotton towels shed lint that the microfiber grabs and holds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Drying the Head</h3>

          <p>
            Drying matters as much as washing. Lay the head flat or hang it; O-Cedar specifically says not to tumble dry on high heat, because heat can melt or stiffen the fine fibers. Rinse and empty the bucket after every use too, and leave it apart to dry. On a two-tank bucket, that means draining the clean tank and leaving its cap and valve open so it can air out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Replace Heads and Pads</h3>

          <p>
            Plan on replacing spin heads regularly. O-Cedar suggests about every three months, or each season, for normal use. Flat pads usually last longer because they are washed more gently and do less heavy scrubbing; Costco's listing for the Bona pad says it can be washed up to 500 times. Either way, a head that stays grey after washing, feels stiff or leaves streaks on a clean floor is done.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Head care at a glance</p>
