        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Mop Without Streaks or Sticky Floors
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Start With a Dry Pass</h3>

          <p>
            Start with a dry pass. Vacuum or sweep first, including along baseboards, because a wet mop turns loose grit into a gritty paste. A cordless stick vacuum makes this a five-minute job; see our <a href="/best-cordless-vacuum/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best cordless vacuum</a> picks if you do not have one. Then fill the bucket with warm water and the smallest dose of cleaner the label allows. More soap does not mean cleaner; it means more film left behind.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Work Toward the Door</h3>

          <p>
            Start at the far side of the room and work back toward the door so you are not walking on the wet floor. O-Cedar recommends an S or figure-eight motion, which keeps the leading edge of the head pushing dirt forward rather than dragging it behind. Use the swivel head for corners and along cabinets, where most dirt collects, then cover the open middle of the room.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Rinse and Wring Often</h3>

          <p>
            Rinse and wring often. On a spin mop, that means after every small section, roughly every 50 to 100 square feet depending on how dirty the floor is. A head that has been on the floor too long is just a sponge full of dirty water. On a flat mop, flip or swap the pad as soon as it looks grey. If the floor dries with streaks, the usual causes are too much cleaner, dirty water, or a pad still holding softener residue.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Let It Dry Fully</h3>

          <p>
            Let the floor dry fully before walking on it, especially wood. If it stays visibly wet for more than a couple of minutes, your head is too wet and needs more spins. On very sticky floors, a final pass with a head rinsed in plain water and wrung hard lifts leftover cleaner and is the simplest fix for a floor that feels tacky under socks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. What Is Not Worth Buying
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Unbranded Kits Without Refill Part Numbers</h3>

          <p>
            Skip unbranded spin mop kits that cannot tell you the refill part number. The bucket is cheap to make; the refills are where you will spend money for years. If a kit has no clear refill listing, no stated head size and no maker support page, you may end up replacing the whole system when the first head wears out. All three spin mops here have refills sold under their own names at major retailers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bacteria-Kill Marketing</h3>

          <p>
            Do not pay extra for bacteria-kill marketing on a mop. Microfiber and water physically remove a lot of germs from a hard floor, which is useful, but a mop is not a disinfectant. If you need to disinfect, use a registered disinfectant and follow its stated wet contact time, then mop with clean water if the label calls for a rinse. That is the label's job, not the mop's.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Disposable-Pad Spray Mops</h3>

          <p>
            Be cautious with disposable-pad spray mops if you mop often. They are convenient, but the pads and proprietary cleaner bottles add up, and many lock you into one brand's solution. A refillable spray mop like the Rubbermaid Reveal lets you choose your own cleaner and wash the pad, which is cheaper over a year and produces less waste.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Wrong Tool for the Floor</h3>

          <p>
            Finally, do not buy a spin mop to fix a carpet or rug problem, or a steam mop to fix a hardwood problem. A spin mop belongs on hard floors only, and steam is not approved by many wood and laminate makers. For rugs and wall-to-wall carpet, the right tool is a carpet cleaner or extractor, which is a different category entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

