        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best spin mop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the O-Cedar EasyWring RinseClean is the best spin mop because its two-tank bucket keeps clean and dirty water separate, so you are not spreading grey rinse water. The original O-Cedar EasyWring is the better value if you will change the water mid-job, and the Libman Tornado suits people who prefer pressing down to pumping a pedal.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are spin mops good for hardwood floors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They can be, if you wring the head nearly dry and keep moving. O-Cedar lists its spin mops for sealed hardwood. The risk is standing water in the seams, which can cup boards and dull the finish. For homes with mostly wood floors, a flat spray mop like the Bona Premium is the safer everyday choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a spin mop better than a flat mop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better everywhere. A spin mop carries more water and a strand head that handles sticky spills, grout and muddy tile better. A flat mop uses less water, reaches under furniture and is gentler on hardwood and laminate. Many homes use a spin mop for kitchens and bathrooms and a flat mop for wood living areas.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I replace a spin mop head?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                O-Cedar recommends replacing its spin mop heads about every three months, or once a season, with normal use. Replace sooner if the head stays grey after washing, feels stiff, smells after drying or leaves streaks on a clean floor. Washing without softener or bleach and air drying it helps a head last its full life.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a spin mop head in the washing machine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. The O-Cedar and Libman heads are machine washable. O-Cedar specifies a warm, gentle cycle with no bleach or fabric softener, then laying the head flat or hanging it to dry rather than tumble drying on high heat. Wash microfiber with other microfiber, since cotton lint clings to it and reduces its pickup.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my floor feel sticky after mopping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually too much cleaner, dirty rinse water, or both. Leftover soap dries into a tacky film that also attracts dirt. Use the smallest label dose, change the water often or use a two-tank bucket, and finish with a pass of plain water and a hard-wrung head. A head washed in fabric softener can also leave residue.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I put in my spin mop water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Warm water and a small dose of pH-neutral floor cleaner suit most sealed floors. Use a wood-specific cleaner on hardwood and a pH-neutral one on natural stone like marble, which can etch. O-Cedar says its RinseClean works with any floor cleaning solution or plain water. Avoid vinegar and oil soap on wood finishes.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mopping Wood With a Wet Head</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A spin mop wrung three times is still too wet for sealed hardwood or laminate. Water left in the seams raises grain and swells laminate cores. Spin until the head is barely damp to the touch, keep moving, and if the floor stays wet for more than a couple of minutes, wring harder.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Fabric Softener on Microfiber</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Softener and dryer sheets coat microfiber so it slides over dirt instead of grabbing it. Bleach weakens the fibers. Wash heads and pads warm on a gentle cycle with plain detergent, away from cotton towels, and air dry them. One softener wash can noticeably reduce a head's cleaning power.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rinsing and Reloading From Dirty Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                In a single bucket, grey water goes straight back onto the floor. That film is why freshly mopped floors look dull or feel tacky. Change the water at the halfway point of any job bigger than one room, keep a second clean bucket, or use a two-tank spin system.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding More Soap to Clean Better</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Extra cleaner does not lift more dirt; it leaves residue that attracts more dirt. Use the smallest dose on the label, or plain warm water for light jobs. If floors feel sticky, do a final pass with a head rinsed in plain water and wrung hard to lift leftover cleaner.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mopping Before Vacuuming</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A wet mop turns grit, crumbs and hair into a paste that smears and can scratch finishes. Vacuum or sweep first, including edges and under furniture. It takes five minutes and makes the mop pass faster, because the head is collecting film and spills, not loose debris.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

